We claim the answer is  $\boxed{\alpha = 4}$ <span style="color:#f00">**Construction:**</span> 
Take  $a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,\ldots = 1,1,2,4,8,\ldots$ where  $a_1=1$ , and  $a_n=2^{n-2}$  for all  $n\ge 2$ . This works since for  $n=1$  we have  $a_1+a_2< 4a_1$  and for  $n\ge 2$ , we have  $$ a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{n+1} = 1+1+2+4+\cdots + 2^{n-1} = 2^n = 4 a_n. $$  <span style="color:#f00">**Bound:**</span> Proof that  $\alpha \ge 4$ .
Assume  $\alpha$  and positive reals  $a_1,a_2,\ldots$  satisfy  $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{n+1} \le \alpha a_n$ .

Define  $d_i = \frac{a_{i+1}}{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_i}$  for positive integers  $i$ .

Observe that   $$ \alpha \ge \frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{n+2} }{a_{n+1}}= \frac{\dfrac{a_1+a_2+\dots+a_{n+1}}{a_1+a_2+\dots+a_n} \cdot \dfrac{a_1+a_2+\dots +a_{n+2} }{a_1+a_2+\dots + a_{n+1}} }{ \dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_1+a_2+\cdots + a_n}  } = \frac{ (d_n+1)(d_{n+1}+1)}{d_n}. $$  By multiplying the first  $m$  inequalities, and noting that  $(x+1)^2\ge 4x$  by AM-GM,
\begin{align*}
\alpha &\ge \frac{(d_1+1)(d_2+1)}{d_1} 
\alpha &\ge \frac{(d_2+1)(d_3+1)}{d_2} 
&\vdots
\alpha &\ge \frac{(d_m+1)(d_{m+1}+1)}{d_m} 
\end{align*}
we obtain   $$ \alpha^m \ge (d_1+1)(d_{m+1}+1)\frac{(d_2+1)^2}{d_2}\cdot \dots \cdot \frac{(d_m+1)^2}{d_m}\ge 1\cdot 1\cdot 4^{m-1} $$  Since  $\alpha^m\ge 4^{m-1}$ , we have  $\alpha \ge 4^{1-\frac{1}{m}}$  for all positive integers  $m$ .
Hence,  $\alpha \ge 4$ , as desired.

So, from the construction and bound, it is proven that  $\alpha=4$  is the smallest that satisfies.

