The answer is  $\boxed{n=p+1}$ . We will demonstrate that this is the optimal value.**<span style="color:#3D85C6">Construction</span>**
Consider the placement defined as follows:


- Place the number  $i+j$  in  $a_{i,j}$  for each integers  $1\leqslant i<j\leqslant p$ .
- Place the number  $0$  in  $a_{(p+1),(p+1)}$ .
- Place the number  $1$  in any other cells that didn't mention above.

Here is an example for  $p=5$ :
[asy]
    size(4cm);

for (int i=0; i<=6; ++i) {
  draw((0,i)--(6,i));
  draw((i,0)--(i,6));
}

for (int i=0; i<6; ++i) {
  for (int j=0; j<6; ++j) {
    pair pos = (j+0.5,i+0.5);

    if (i==0 && j==5) {
      label(" $0$ ",pos);
    }
    else if (i==0 || j==5) {
      label(" $1$ ",pos);
    }
    else {
      int lcle = 6-i+j;
      label(" $"+(string)lcle+"$ ",pos);
    }
  }
}
[/asy]
One could easily see that 
\begin{align*}
a_{i,k}a_{j,l}-a_{i,l}a_{j,k}&=(i+k)(j+l)-(i+l)(j+k)
&=ij+il+kj+kl-ij-ik-jl-kl
&=il+kj-ik-jl
&=(i-j)(l-k).
\end{align*}
Which can't be divisible by  $p$  due to uniqueness of  $1\leqslant i<j\leqslant p$  and  $1\leqslant l<k\leqslant p$  in modulo  $p$ .
Notice that we have
\begin{align*}
    a_{i,k}a_{j,(p+1)}-a_{i,(p+1)}a_{j,k}=(i+k)\cdot 1-(j+k)\cdot 1=i-j
    a_{i,k}a_{(p+1),l}-a_{i,l}a_{(p+1),k}=(i+k)\cdot 1-(i+l)\cdot 1=k-l
    a_{i,k}a_{(p+1),(p+1)}-a_{i,(p+1)}a_{(p+1),k}=0-1=-1
\end{align*}
for all  $1\leqslant i<j\leqslant p$ , and  $1\leqslant k<l\leqslant n$ . Which is clear that these three equations isn't divisible by  $p$ .
So that our construction is valid.**<span style="color:#E69138">Bounding</span>**
Observe that we can't have two integer  $a\equiv b\equiv 0\pmod{p}$  lying on the same rows or columns.
Suppose that  $n\geqslant p+2$ . From now on, we may only consider integers in  $\mathbb{F}_p$ . 
We choose two different rows, then remove the columns that contains  $0$ . This results in  $n-2$  columns.
Let  $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_N$  be the numbers in the first row, and  $b_1,b_2,\dots,b_N$  be the numbers in the second row. Note that  $a_ib_i^{-1}$  could leave a residue of  $1,2,\dots,p-1$  modulo  $p$ . By pigeonhole principle, and since  $N\geqslant n-2\geqslant p$ , there exists  $1\leqslant i< j\leqslant N$  such that   $$ a_ib_i^{-1}\equiv a_jb_j^{-1}\pmod {p}\implies a_ib_j\equiv a_jb_i\pmod{p}. $$  Which violates our condition of  $p\nmid a_{ik}a_{jl}-a_{il}a_{jk}$ .

