It's pretty easy to solve with complex numbers. It suffices to show that the points  $i, r_1, r_2, r_1r_2 \cdot i$  are concyclic. That follows from 
\begin{align*} \frac{r_1-i}{r_2-i} : \frac{r_1-r_1r_2i}{r_2-r_1r_2i} &= \frac{(r_1-i)(r_2-r_1r_2i)}{(r_2-i)(r_1-r_1r_2i)}
 &= \frac{r_1r_2-i \left(r_2+r_1^2r_2 \right)-r_1r_2}{r_1r_2-i \left(r_1+r_1r_2^2 \right)-r_1r_2}
 &= \frac{r_2+r_1^2r_2}{r_1+r_1r_2^2} \in \mathbb{R}.
\end{align*} Done.  $\hfill \square$

Here are two approaches. In both solutions we orient the loop counterclockwise, and for simplicity we assume the loop has length  $1$ .
-------------------
<span style="color:blue">**Solution by induction.**</span> We proceed by induction on  $n \ge 1$  with the base case  $n=1$  being clear.
For  $n \ge 2$ , the  $n$  tanks divide the circle into  $n$  arcs whose lengths sum to  $1$ . Because the total gas in the tanks is also  $1$ , there should be a pair of adjacent tanks  $t$  and  $t'$  such that one can reach  $t'$  using only the gas from  $t$ . (There could be multiple such pairs; pick arbitrarily.)
Now imagine removing  $t'$  and adding its gas to  $t$  instead. An example of this process is shown below:
[asy]
size(12cm); dotfactor *= 1.5;
picture orig, reduced; draw(orig, unitcircle); draw(reduced, unitcircle); real a = 360 * 0.16; real b = 360 * 0.22 + a; real c = 360 * 0.63 + a; real d = a + 360; pair A = dir(a); pair B = dir(b); pair C = dir(c);
dot(orig, " $0.3$  gas", A, A, blue); dot(orig, " $0.2$  gas", B, B, blue); dot(orig, " $0.5$  gas", C, C, blue); label(orig, " $0.22$  km", dir((a+b)/2), dir((a+b)/2)); label(orig, " $0.41$  km", dir((b+c)/2), dir((b+c)/2)); label(orig, " $0.37$  km", dir((c+d)/2), dir((c+d)/2)); label(orig, " $t$ ", A, -A, red); label(orig, " $t'$ ", B, -B, red);
dot(reduced, " $0.5$  gas", A, A, blue); dot(reduced, " $0.5$  gas", C, C, blue); label(reduced, " $0.63$  km", dir((a+c)/2), dir((a+c)/2)); label(reduced, " $0.37$  km", dir((c+d)/2), dir((c+d)/2)); label(reduced, " $t$ ", A, -A, red);
add(orig); add(shift(4,0)*reduced);
[/asy]
Applying the induction hypothesis to this configuration of  $n-1$  tanks gives that there is some valid journey for these  $n-1$  tanks starting somewhere (not necessarily starting at  $t$ ). Any journey on these  $n-1$  tanks is then a valid journey on  $n$  tanks.
-------------------
<span style="color:blue">**Solution by cyclic shift method.**</span> Pick an arbitrary starting point  $P$  (not necessarily at a tank) and imagine having a car complete multiple laps starting from  $P$ , allowing it to have negative gas, and graphing its total gas as a function of distance/time. We still know that each time the car passes  $P$ , its gas should be  $0$ . An example of this process is shown below, with the first lap drawn in a bold red, and the same pattern then continuing indefinitely.

[asy]
size(14cm); dotfactor *= 1.5; picture orig, reduced; draw(orig, unitcircle); real a = 360 * 0.16; real b = 360 * 0.22 + a; real c = 360 * 0.63 + a; real d = a + 360; pair A = dir(a); pair B = dir(b); pair C = dir(c);
dot(orig, " $0.3$  gas", A, A, blue); dot(orig, " $0.2$  gas", B, B, blue); dot(orig, " $0.5$  gas", C, C, blue); label(orig, " $0.16$  km", dir(a/2), dir(a/2)); label(orig, " $0.22$  km", dir((a+b)/2), dir((a+b)/2)); label(orig, " $0.41$  km", dir((b+c)/2), dir((b+c)/2)); label(orig, " $0.21$  km", dir((c+360)/2), dir((c+360)/2)); dot(orig, " $P$  (start)", dir(0), dir(0), deepgreen);
add(shift(-1.5,0)*scale(0.6)*orig);
path g = (0,0)--(0.16,-0.16)--(0.16,0.14)--(0.38,-0.08)--(0.38,0.12)--(0.79,-0.29)--(0.79,0.21)--(1,0); label(" $+0.3$ ", (0.16,0.14), dir(100), blue); label(" $+0.2$ ", (0.38,0.12), dir(80), blue); label(" $+0.5$ ", (0.79,0.21), dir(90), blue);
draw((0,0)--(2.2,0), EndArrow); draw((0,-0.6)--(0,0.6), Arrows); draw(shift(1,0)*g, gray); draw(g, red + 1.3); label("Gas", (0,0.6), dir(135)); dot((1,0)); dot((2,0)); real h = 0.5; label(" $1$  km", (1,-h)); label(" $2$  km", (2,-h)); draw((1,0)--(1,-h), gray, Margins); draw((2,0)--(2,-h), gray, Margins); dot(" $0$ ", (0,0), dir(225)); draw((-0.2,-0.29)--(2.2,-0.29), purple+dashed, Arrows); dot((0.79,-0.29), purple); dot((1.79,-0.29), purple);
[/asy]
Now consider the lowest vertical points in the graph and draw a line through them. Then starting from one of these points gives the desired starting point.**<span style="color:red">Remark:</span>** The second solution also implies that the starting point is usually *unique*. More formally, if either the tank positions or tank values are chosen randomly, then almost certainly there will be a unique lowest point (i.e.\ the bottom-most purple points above will really have period  $1$ ), and hence there is exactly one tank that can be started with. Thus in my opinion the second solution is more informative.
